# Accepted-risk findings for Trivy's ansible-misconfig/secret scans —
# see docs/security-scanning.md#trivy-security-scans. One shared file, referenced by
# both scan jobs' `trivyignores:` input via its .config/ path — same
# location as this repo's other tool configs (.ansible-lint, .yamllint,
# .dclintrc).
#
# One entry per line: an AVD/check ID (Ansible misconfig scan) or a
# secret rule ID (secret scan). Blank lines and full-line '#' comments
# are ignored when Trivy parses this file — same convention as
# .github/compose-boot-test-exclusions.txt. Attach the reasoning for
# each entry directly above it, matching that file's style, rather than
# tracking justifications elsewhere.
#
# ansible/roles/seaweedfs_bucket/tasks/main.yaml's amazon.aws.s3_bucket
# task sets public_access to satisfy these, targeting SeaweedFS (pinned
# ghcr.io/chrislusf/seaweedfs:4.47, see docker/seaweedfs/compose.yaml.j2)
# via endpoint_url. Confirmed live against that exact pinned image: a
# PutPublicAccessBlock call returns "NotImplemented: A header you
# provided implies functionality that is not implemented" — there's no
# working API on this version to set these against, so adding the
# parameter would only add a task that fails at apply time. SeaweedFS's
# own S3 API compatibility docs list this operation as supported in
# their current release, so this is a version gap, not a permanent one
# — revisit if the pinned image is ever upgraded. `exp:` set to three
# months out rather than left open-ended, so this comes back for a
# fresh re-check on a homelab-appropriate cadence instead of silently
# riding along forever. `<ID> exp:YYYY-MM-DD` confirmed live against
# this exact Trivy version (0.74.0): a past date lets the finding fire
# again, a future date suppresses it — this isn't documented in
# Trivy's own --help output, so confirmed against actual behavior
# rather than assumed from the flag name.
AWS-0086 exp:2026-12-22
AWS-0087 exp:2026-12-22
AWS-0091 exp:2026-12-22
AWS-0093 exp:2026-12-22

# Same task and same pinned SeaweedFS image as above. Confirmed live: a
# PutBucketLogging call doesn't return a clean "unsupported" error — it
# falls through to the bucket-creation path and returns
# "BucketAlreadyOwnedByYou", so there's no working write side to
# configure. Matches SeaweedFS's own compatibility docs, which list
# GetBucketLogging as supported (always returns an empty status) but
# PutBucketLogging as not implemented at all. Same three-month `exp:`
# as the entries above, for the same reason.
AWS-0089 exp:2026-12-22
